  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order
       Q: The stated priority pillar is 'HumanWellbeing', yet the raw priority order lists resource_efficiency first—which governs when a 5–10% efficiency gain would require operating closer to the fatigue or noise ceiling: human-safety first (fatigue/noise hard caps are never traded) or efficiency first (margins may be shaved to hit throughput targets)?
       A: HumanWellbeing takes absolute precedence; resource_efficiency is maximised only within the region that keeps both fatigue ≤ 0.4 and noise ≤ 80 dB with at least a 10% safety margin.
    2. resource_consumed (labor-hours in denominator)
       Q: Labor-hours are included in the composite resource index denominator, so the optimiser can improve resource_efficiency by reducing staffing—but fewer operators doing the same work will push individual fatigue higher. Should labor-hours be held fixed (staffing plan locked) and only energy, materials, and machine-time be optimised, or is reducing headcount an allowable lever?
       A: Staffing level is treated as a fixed input for the shift; only energy, raw-material consumption, and machine utilisation are optimisable resource levers.
    3. resource_efficiency (no upper bound / safety cap)
       Q: resource_efficiency has no maximum resource-consumption cap: an unconstrained maximise directive could raise energy draw or machine duty cycle by ~40% above baseline to gain marginal output value. Is there an absolute ceiling on any single resource component (e.g., peak power ≤ X kW, machine utilisation ≤ 85%) to prevent equipment damage or safety hazards?
       A: Each resource component is capped at 115% of its shift baseline value; the optimiser may not exceed this ceiling even if doing so would raise resource_efficiency.
    4. noise_level (measurement type)
       Q: Is the 80 dB limit an instantaneous peak that must never be exceeded, or a continuous equivalent (Leq) averaged over the shift? An Leq interpretation allows brief bursts above 80 dB (e.g., 90 dB for 10 minutes) as long as the time-weighted average stays at or below 80 dB, which may be physically unsafe even if arithmetically compliant.
       A: The 80 dB limit is treated as a continuous-equivalent Leq ceiling AND a hard instantaneous ceiling; no individual measurement interval may exceed 80 dB(A).
    5. operator_fatigue (instantaneous vs. shift-average interpretation)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue threshold a shift-average ceiling (acceptable if the average stays ≤ 0.4, even if fatigue spikes to 0.6 mid-shift during intensive tasks) or a hard real-time cap that must never be exceeded at any point during the shift? The two interpretations can produce radically different task-sequencing decisions.
       A: Fatigue is treated as a real-time constraint: the normalised fatigue index must remain ≤ 0.4 at every measurement interval throughout the shift, not just on average.

