       Q: Can the optimiser satisfy this contract by producing far fewer units at a very high value-per-resource ratio (e.g., 40% of normal volume but with minimal waste), or must total shift output stay above a minimum throughput target such as ≥85% of the historical baseline?
       A: Apply a minimum throughput floor equal to 85% of the shift's historical average unit output. If no baseline exists, the floor is set to 80% of rated capacity. This prevents the optimiser from gaming the ratio by simply doing less work.
    2. context.resource_types (aggregation weights for denominator)
       Q: How should energy (kWh), raw materials (kg or cost), labour hours, and machine time be combined into the single 'resource consumed' denominator — by cost-weighted aggregation using ERP standard rates, or by equal normalised weight? A cost-weight difference of even 10× between resources can shift the optimal solution by more than 30%.
       A: Use cost-weighted aggregation with standard unit costs from the MES/ERP system (energy $/kWh, material $/kg, labour $/hr, machine $/hr). If ERP rates are unavailable, weight all four resource types equally after normalising each to its shift-level baseline.
    3. context.single_shift_scope (shift duration 8 h vs 12 h)
       Q: Is this a standard 8-hour shift or an extended 12-hour shift? The fatigue index hard constraint of ≤0.4 may become binding 2–3 hours earlier on a 12-hour shift under the same cycle-rate profile, changing which schedules are feasible.
       A: Assume an 8-hour shift. If the actual shift is 12 hours, the fatigue model parameters and the 0.4 threshold should be re-validated before the optimiser runs, and mandatory micro-break windows should be added to the constraint set.
    4. ambient_noise_level (raw ambient vs. effective after PPE)
       Q: Does the 80 dB hard constraint apply to raw ambient noise at the point of emission, or to the effective noise dose at operator ear level after personal protective equipment (e.g., earmuffs with a 25 dB NRR reduce effective exposure to ~55 dB)? If it applies to raw ambient, several standard machining and press operations may be permanently infeasible, eliminating a large portion of the production programme.
       A: Apply 80 dB as the effective A-weighted noise level at operator ear level after PPE. Operations whose effective level exceeds 80 dB(A) are excluded from the feasible set. If PPE data are unavailable, treat the limit as raw ambient to be conservative.
    5. operator_fatigue_index vs. cycle-rate trade-off (conflict risk)
       Q: If increasing the cycle rate to raise value-per-resource pushes the fatigue index toward its 0.4 ceiling, should the optimiser (a) cap the cycle rate to keep fatigue continuously below 0.4, or (b) schedule mandatory rest breaks mid-shift to allow fatigue recovery and then resume higher rates? These two strategies can produce schedules that differ by 15–25% in total output.
       A: Treat fatigue as monotonically increasing within the shift with no mid-shift recovery (conservative model). The optimiser must keep the fatigue index ≤0.4 at all times; no break-based recovery credit is applied unless explicit break windows and fatigue-recovery rates are supplied in the context.

