  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency / context.resource_types
       Q: How are the four resource types (energy, raw materials, labor hours, machine time) combined into a single denominator — are they summed using fixed monetary costs, and if so what are those weights? Without this, the optimiser may trade 1 labour-hour for 50 kWh of energy in a way you did not intend.
       A: Each resource type is converted to cost using standard plant accounting rates (energy: tariff per kWh, materials: BOM unit cost, labour: loaded hourly rate, machine time: asset depreciation rate per hour); all four are summed in the same currency unit to form total_resources_consumed.
    2. resource_efficiency (no minimum throughput floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum output quota (e.g. at least 500 units or £X value) that must be delivered this shift regardless of efficiency? Without one, the optimiser could achieve a very high efficiency ratio by running at near-idle throughput, meeting the ratio target while producing almost nothing.
       A: A minimum throughput floor equal to 80% of the shift's standard production plan target is assumed; feasibility requires delivered value to exceed this floor before efficiency maximisation begins.
    3. operator_fatigue (hard_constraint — peak vs. time-average)
       Q: Must operator fatigue stay at or below 0.4 at every instant throughout the shift, or is the 0.4 limit applied to the shift-average? An average-based rule allows the optimiser to front-load intense work (fatigue briefly hitting ~0.6) then recover — is that acceptable?
       A: The constraint is treated as a never-exceeded instantaneous ceiling: fatigue must remain ≤ 0.4 at all times, with no averaging window permitted.
    4. noise_level (hard_constraint — LAeq averaging vs. instantaneous peak)
       Q: The assumption specifies LAeq (time-averaged) noise ≤ 80 dB(A) over the shift; this permits short bursts above 80 dB that are offset by quieter periods. Is a brief peak — say 88 dB for 5 minutes — acceptable as long as the shift average remains ≤ 80 dB(A), or must noise never exceed 80 dB at any moment?
       A: The 80 dB limit is enforced as a rolling 15-minute LAeq ceiling, never exceeded at any 15-minute window during the shift, to balance practical averaging against instantaneous exposure risk.
    5. priority_order vs. context._raw_priority_order (internal conflict)
       Q: The contract's priority_order lists only 'HumanWellbeing', implying fatigue and noise override everything, while _raw_priority_order lists resource_efficiency first. When efficiency can be improved by driving fatigue or noise to their hard-constraint boundaries (e.g. fatigue = 0.39), should the optimiser push to those limits or maintain a safety buffer — and if a buffer, how large (e.g. 10%)?
       A: HumanWellbeing takes absolute precedence; the optimiser applies a 10% safety margin inside both hard constraints (effective fatigue ceiling = 0.36, effective noise ceiling = 72 dB(A)) and maximises resource_efficiency only within that reduced feasible region.

