    - Option 2 (Moderate Risk): Partial Demand Deferral with Engineering Noise Controls: Defer approximately 10% of the incremental demand to the next planning period, reducing the effective load increase to ~5%. Simultaneously, deploy engineering noise-reduction measures (acoustic baffles, machine dampening) to bring Noise from 84.9 dB toward the 80.0 dB limit. Mandate PPE (ear protection rated ≥ 5 dB NRR) as an interim administrative control and raise the operational Noise ceiling to 82.0 dB for the duration of the engineering upgrade. FatigueIndex remains managed within the original 0.40 limit through the reduced demand volume. The Noise constraint is temporarily relaxed, so this option does not fully preserve human-centric constraints.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (High Risk, Last Resort): Time-Limited Dual Constraint Relaxation with Enhanced Monitoring: Temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.45 (from 0.40) and the Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB (from 80.0 dB) for a strictly bounded window not to exceed 5 consecutive working days. This allows the full demand increase to be met without capacity changes or demand deferral. Mandatory safeguards must be in place before execution: (1) continuous real-time fatigue monitoring with automatic line-stop triggers at 0.48; (2) mandatory hearing-protection PPE for all workers; (3) daily occupational-health check-ins; (4) automatic reversion to original limits after the window expires. Both human-centric constraints are relaxed, representing an elevated risk to worker health and safety. This option requires written sign-off from the safety officer and production manager.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
