    1. resource_efficiency (maximize, threshold: null)
       Q: Is there a ceiling on how aggressively resource efficiency can be pursued—for example, should cycle rates be capped at 100 % of rated machine speed, or is a 20–40 % output surge acceptable if energy and material costs proportionally rise?
       A: Cap resource_efficiency optimisation at the rated production capacity of the slowest bottleneck cell (100 % utilisation ceiling), preventing the solver from scheduling overtime bursts or overclocking equipment to lift the numerator of the efficiency ratio.
    2. operator_fatigue hard_constraint (<=0.4) vs resource_efficiency (maximize)
       Q: Under the current variant mix and cycle-time targets, biomechanical models show that efficiency gains above roughly 15 % require fatigue scores of 0.45–0.55—should the optimiser treat the fatigue hard constraint as inviolable (accepting a lower efficiency ceiling) or can the 0.4 limit be raised to 0.5 when compensating rest breaks are scheduled?
       A: Treat the 0.4 fatigue limit as a hard inviolable constraint; the solver must not exceed it under any efficiency-gain scenario. This may reduce the achievable resource-efficiency gain to below 15 %.
    3. ambient_noise_level measurement method (time-weighted average vs instantaneous peak)
       Q: The 80 dB limit is defined as a weighted average over the 8-hour shift—should transient peaks (e.g. 95 dB bursts lasting <5 minutes from press operations) be explicitly capped at a separate peak threshold (e.g. 85 dB instantaneous), or is the shift-average the only binding measure?
       A: Apply only the 8-hour time-weighted average (TWA) of 80 dB(A) as specified; instantaneous peaks are not separately constrained. Flag this assumption in the compliance report so safety officers can review.
    4. resource_consumption_metric component weights (energy vs materials vs labor-hours)
       Q: The denominator of resource_efficiency aggregates energy, raw materials, and labor-hours—what weighting or common currency (e.g. monetary cost, CO₂-equivalent, or equal normalised units) should be used so that a trade-off between, say, consuming 20 % more energy to reduce labor-hours by 10 % is evaluated correctly?
       A: Use equal-weight normalised units (each resource type scaled to its shift-baseline mean = 1.0) until explicit cost or CO₂ weights are provided. This may cause the solver to favour labour reduction over energy savings in ways that do not reflect actual operational cost priorities.
    5. resource_efficiency minimum throughput floor (no lower bound defined)
       Q: Maximising the ratio of value-out to resource-in could be trivially satisfied by running a single high-margin part and idling the rest of the shift—should a minimum delivered-value floor (e.g. at least 80 % of the planned shift volume) be enforced to prevent the optimiser from 'gaming' efficiency via low-volume cherry-picking?
       A: Enforce a minimum throughput of 80 % of the pre-shift production plan (in standardised value units) as a soft constraint with high penalty weight, ensuring the efficiency ratio is optimised over a realistic production volume rather than a trivially small batch.

