  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency.threshold
       Q: Is there a maximum throughput rate or efficiency ceiling beyond which further gains are not acceptable (e.g., ≤ 120% of baseline output rate)? Without any upper bound, the optimiser will legally push operators to exactly fatigue = 0.4 and machines to exactly 80 dB for the entire shift to maximise efficiency — likely not the manager's intent.
       A: Impose an implicit soft ceiling by targeting fatigue ≤ 0.30 and noise ≤ 75 dB as operating targets, reserving the hard limits (0.4 and 80 dB) as inviolable emergency ceilings, giving a 25 % and 6 % safety buffer respectively.
    2. context.shift_duration / operator_fatigue.threshold interpretation
       Q: What is the planned shift length (6, 8, or 12 hours), and is the fatigue limit of 0.4 a hard ceiling at any instant, a rolling hourly average, or an end-of-shift value? On a 12-hour shift a point-in-time cap of 0.4 may force deep slowdowns from hour 6 onward, collapsing effective efficiency for the second half.
       A: Assume an 8-hour shift and apply fatigue = 0.4 as a hard ceiling at every decision epoch (most conservative reading); if actual shift duration is longer, the constraint will trigger replanning automatically.
    3. noise_level.direction (KPI minimize vs. hard constraint ceiling)
       Q: Should noise be actively minimised below 80 dB wherever possible (competing with efficiency maximisation), or is 80 dB simply a hard ceiling and the optimiser is free to run at 78–79 dB if that maximises efficiency? Treating noise as both a minimise-KPI and a hard constraint creates an unnecessary secondary objective that silently steals efficiency headroom.
       A: Treat 80 dB solely as a hard upper-bound constraint, not as an active minimisation target; the optimiser may operate at any noise level ≤ 80 dB and should use that headroom in service of efficiency.
    4. resource_efficiency (weighting / aggregation of resource types)
       Q: How should energy, raw materials, machine time, and labour hours be weighted against each other in the efficiency denominator? For example, if cutting energy by 10 % requires 20 % more raw material, is that a net efficiency gain or loss? Without explicit weights the optimiser may make resource substitutions the manager would find unacceptable.
       A: Weight each resource type by its current monetary cost (EUR or USD per unit), normalising to a single cost-per-unit-of-conforming-output metric; ties broken in favour of reducing energy consumption.
    5. kpi_targets / priority_order (missing minimum output volume floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum production volume (e.g., order-fulfilment target or percentage of prior equivalent shift) that must be met regardless of efficiency calculations? A pure efficiency maximiser may legally reduce total output if a lower volume can be delivered more resource-efficiently — gaming the KPI without meeting customer demand.
       A: Enforce a minimum throughput floor of 90 % of the rolling 4-shift average for the same shift type; if no historical data are available, set the floor to the daily order-book demand divided by number of shifts, rounded up.

